---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { models, providers, updatedAt, sourceUrl } from '../lib/models';

const exactCount = models.filter((m) => m.counting === 'exact').length;
const tierCount = models.filter((m) => m.tier).length;
---

<Base
  title="About Token Ledger — how the pricing data is built"
  description={`Where the prices come from, how often they update, and which token counts are exact. ${models.length} models across ${providers.length} providers, synced ${updatedAt}.`}
  breadcrumbs={[['About', '/about/']]}
>
  <section class="wrap page">
    <span class="eyebrow">Methodology</span>
    <h1>How this site is built</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Token Ledger answers one question: what a specific prompt costs across
      every model you might send it to. Everything below is how that number is
      produced, so you can decide how much to trust it.
    </p>

    <h2>Where prices come from</h2>
    <p>
      Rates are pulled from the <a href={sourceUrl} rel="nofollow noopener">LiteLLM
      pricing dataset</a>, an open, community-maintained record of published API
      rates. The sync runs as a script, not by hand, so a model added upstream
      appears here without anyone remembering to add it. The date at the bottom
      of every page is the last successful sync — currently <strong class="mono">{updatedAt}</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Figures are list prices per million tokens. They exclude batch discounts,
      negotiated enterprise rates, free tiers, and anything a reseller might
      charge on top. Where a provider bills differently past a context threshold,
      that second rate is shown too — <strong>{tierCount}</strong> of the
      {' '}{models.length} models here have one, and quoting only the base rate
      would understate long prompts by up to 2×.
    </p>

    <h2>Which token counts are exact</h2>
    <p>
      Only OpenAI publishes the tokenizer its models bill against. Counts for
      those <strong>{exactCount}</strong> models are produced by running that
      encoder on your text, in your browser. Every other family — Anthropic,
      Google, Mistral, and the rest — has no public tokenizer, so their counts
      are approximations derived from published ratios.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those two cases look identical on most comparison sites. Here every model
      carries an <span class="badge badge--exact">exact</span> or
      {' '}<span class="badge badge--estimated">estimated</span> badge, because a
      cost estimate built on a silent guess is worse than one you know to
      double-check.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not tracked</h2>
    <p>
      Your prompt never leaves your browser. Tokenizing happens client-side and
      nothing is uploaded, logged, or stored. See the
      {' '}<a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for the full picture.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Prices change constantly and upstream data occasionally lags a provider's
      own announcement. If a figure here disagrees with a provider's pricing
      page, the provider is right — <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> and it gets
      fixed in the next sync.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-top: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 1rem 0 2.5rem;
    max-width: 58ch;
  }
  h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  p strong {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
